course: dictionary
lesson: 10811
title: Vast: very, very big
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how big something is. Vast is one of them. When you meet it, ask yourself: is this thing bigger than I can easily picture? «Jupiter and Saturn pulled in vast amounts of hydrogen and helium gas.» Those gases were so much that the word vast tells you the amount was enormous.
explain: The word vast can describe size, amount or area. «A vast shell of icy bodies» is a huge space around the Solar System, and «a vast span of Earth's history» is a time far longer than people have lived on Earth. You can test your meaning by swapping in huge: «a huge span of Earth's history» still makes sense, so huge is a good clue.
explain: Vast also has a special use in numbers. «The vast majority» means nearly all, not just a lot. If a text says the vast majority of scientists agree, it means almost every scientist agrees. Read the words around vast to see whether it is talking about size or about a big number of people or things.
fact: «Vast» means extremely large in size, amount or area.
fact: «The vast majority» means nearly all.
fact: Good synonyms for vast are huge, enormous and immense.
fact: «A tsunami is a series of huge waves made when something suddenly shoves a vast amount of sea water.»
fact: «Seeing how vast the universe is can make worries feel smaller, and many people find it calming.»
exercise: «Jupiter and Saturn pulled in vast amounts of hydrogen and helium gas.» What does vast mean here? | *Very large in amount | Very small | Very quiet | Very cold
exercise: «Geologists call the vast span of Earth's history deep time.» Which word is the closest in meaning to vast? | *huge | tiny | short | quiet
exercise: A survey says «the vast majority» of children like football. What does that mean? | Exactly half of the children | *Nearly all of the children | Only a few children | None of the children
test: «The vast desert stretched as far as the eye could see.» What does vast tell us about the desert? | It was very cold | *It was very large in area | It was very small | It was very noisy
test: «Seeing how vast the universe is can make worries feel smaller.» Why might worries feel smaller? | *Because the universe is so big that worries seem small by comparison | Because the universe is loud and busy | Because worries grow when the sky is dark | Because worries only happen at night
exam: «A tsunami is a series of huge waves made when something suddenly shoves a vast amount of sea water.» What does vast amount mean? | A warm amount | Exactly one drop | Very small amount | *Very large amount
